The Mercedes-Benz C-Class C300e is a plug-in hybrid petrol variant of the current W206 generation. It combines a petrol engine with an electric motor and a large battery, focusing on efficiency and electric-only capability for shorter trips.

Here's an evaluation based on the provided data and scoring guide:

*   **price\_usd**: Plug-in hybrid variants are typically more expensive than standard internal combustion engine models. UK prices for the C300e range from £49,860 - £58,860 (approx $62,000 - $74,000 USD) (Source [1]). This is significantly above the $35,000 market average. Score: -0.60.
*   **total\_cost\_estimate\_5yr\_usd**: High purchase price, potential for higher maintenance on a complex hybrid system, and potentially higher insurance costs contribute to a high 5-year cost of ownership, although significant fuel savings from the hybrid system and potential tax benefits (Source [1]) could offset some costs. CarEdge estimates ~$3,488 for maintenance/repair in the first five years for the C-Class generally, which is less than the industry average for luxury sedans (Source [37]). However, this doesn't account for the PHEV complexity or depreciation. Score: -0.50.
*   **insurance\_usd\_yr**: Luxury vehicles, especially those with complex powertrains and higher repair costs, typically have higher insurance rates. Score: -0.20.
*   **maint\_usd\_yr**: Mercedes-Benz maintenance is generally higher than average (Source [18, 37]). The added complexity of a plug-in hybrid system could lead to higher maintenance costs compared to a standard ICE model (Source [11]). Score: -0.70.
*   **repair\_risk\_score**: The W206 C-Class has received some reports of issues, particularly with the MBUX infotainment system and some electronic glitches (Source [11]). The complexity of the plug-in hybrid system adds potential points of failure (Source [11]). CarEdge rates the C-Class as worse than average for major repair probability over 10 years (Source [37]). Score: -0.40.
*   **hp**: Total system output is 308-330 hp (Source [1, 4, 8, 10, 22, 24]). This is well above the 200 hp average. Score: 0.60.
*   **torque**: Total system torque is 550 Nm (406 lb-ft) (Source [4, 10, 24]). Some sources list 350 Nm (258 lb-ft) for the engine only (Source [29]). Using the higher system torque. This is significantly above the 190 lb-ft average. Score: 0.80.
*   **cylinder\_count**: The internal combustion engine is a 4-cylinder petrol (Source [1, 4, 8, 10, 22, 24]). This is common, so average. Score: 0.00.
*   **engine\_displacement**: The internal combustion engine is 2.0L (1991 cc or 1999 cc) (Source [1, 4, 8, 10, 22, 24]). Around the average for a 4-cylinder engine. Score: 0.00.
*   **accel\_0\_60\_sec**: Around 5.5-6.2 seconds (0-62 mph) (Source [1, 4, 8, 10, 22, 29]). This is significantly faster than the 7.5-second average. Score: 0.70.
*   **top\_speed**: Around 149-155 mph (Source [1, 8, 22, 29]). Around the 150 mph average. Score: 0.00.
*   **drive\_type**: RWD is standard (Source [8, 10, 29]), AWD (4Matic) may be available in some markets. RWD scores +1.00 according to the guide. Score: 1.00.
*   **transmission\_type**: 9-speed automatic (9G-TRONIC) (Source [1, 4, 8, 10, 22, 29]). This is a modern, smooth automatic. Score: 1.00.
*   **offroad\_ability**: Low ground clearance (around 9.7 cm or 3.8 inches) (Source [9]), sedan/estate body style, not designed for off-road. Score: 1.00 (based on guide examples).
*   **braking\_dist\_ft**: Car and Driver measured 154 ft from 70 mph for the C300 (Source [32]). This is worse than the 125 ft average from 60 mph. Score: -0.50.
*   **size**: Length is around 4702-4751 mm (185-187 inches) (Source [9, 22, 29]). This is slightly larger than the 4600mm average. Score: 0.10.
*   **wheelbase**: 2840-2865 mm (111.8-112.8 inches) (Source [9, 29]). This is longer than the 2700mm average. Score: 0.20.
*   **body\_style**: 4-door Sedan (Source [9, 22]). Estate also available (Source [1, 29]). Score: 0.00.
*   **seats**: 5 seats (Source [21, 29, 32]). Score: 0.00.
*   **cargo\_space**: 315 liters (11.12 cu ft) for the sedan (Source [1, 10, 20, 29]). The large battery significantly reduces trunk space compared to the standard C-Class sedan (455 liters / 16 cu ft) (Source [10, 20]). This is below the 15 cu ft average. Score: -0.50.
*   **rear\_space**: Improved over the previous generation, with more legroom (Source [1, 12, 23]). Score: 0.20.
*   **ground\_clearance\_in**: Around 9.7 cm (3.8 inches) (Source [9]). Low for a sedan by modern standards. Score: -0.20.
*   **turn\_radius\_ft**: 11.07m (36.32 ft) for RWD (Source [15, 22, 29]), 10.64m (34.9 ft) with optional rear-axle steering (Source [23]). Around the 36 ft average. Score: 0.00.
*   **mpg**: Plug-in hybrids have very high claimed fuel economy figures when driven on a mix of electric and fuel power. Combined WLTP/NEDC around 0.5-0.7 L/100 km (equivalent to extremely high MPG, e.g., 404-565 MPG UK) (Source [1, 5, 7, 24]). This is well above the 30 mpg average for a conventional car. Score: 1.00.
*   **co2\_g\_km**: Very low CO2 emissions due to significant electric driving capability. WLTP is around 12-24 g/km (Source [1, 5, 7, 10, 24]). This is well below the 175 g/km average. Score: 0.90.
*   **electric\_range\_mi**: WLTP electric range is around 62-71 miles (93-116 km) (Source [1, 5, 7, 10, 12, 25, 31]). This is a very good electric range for a PHEV. Score: 0.80.
*   **charge\_speed\_kw**: Supports AC charging up to 11 kW (Source [14, 25, 31]) and optional DC fast charging up to 55 kW (Source [4, 10, 14]). 11 kW AC is good, 55 kW DC is decent for a PHEV but not as fast as dedicated EVs. Score: 0.40.
*   **style\_sleek\_vs\_boxy**: Modern sedan design, leaning towards sleek, inspired by the S-Class (Source [2, 9, 26, 34]). Score: 0.40.
*   **style\_aggression\_rating**: Standard C-Class styling is elegant, AMG Line adds aggression (Source [26, 35]). Assuming standard C300e styling. Score: 0.00.
*   **style\_modernity\_rating**: Recent design, looks modern and inspired by the S-Class (Source [2, 9, 26, 34]). Score: 0.80.
*   **handling\_rating**: Capable, but reviews suggest it's focused more on comfort than sporty handling compared to rivals (Source [1, 2, 3, 12, 26, 27]). The added weight of the battery (around 2080-2120kg kerb weight) (Source [1, 4]) can impact agility (Source [1, 8]). Score: -0.10.
*   **ride\_comfort\_rating**: Generally praised for excellent ride comfort, a significant improvement over the W205 (Source [1, 2, 3, 12, 20, 26, 27]). PHEV models often have standard rear air suspension (Source [1, 4, 20]), enhancing ride. Score: 0.80.
*   **cabin\_noise\_rating**: Praised for a quiet and refined cabin, especially when running on electric power (Source [1, 6, 20]). Score: 0.80.
*   **shift\_smooth\_rating**: 9G-Tronic is generally smooth (Source [1, 2, 3, 6, 8, 26]). Integration between the engine and electric motor can sometimes be noticeable, but generally well-executed (Source [8]). Score: 0.50.
*   **steering\_feel\_rating**: Reviews mention a lack of steering feel despite being precise (Source [1, 2, 3, 12, 26, 27]). Score: -0.50.
*   **adas\_score**: Offers a wide range of modern ADAS features, especially with optional packages (Source [23, 35]). Score: 0.60.
*   **visibility\_rating**: Standard sedan visibility, reviews don't highlight major issues (Source [20]). Score: 0.00.
*   **headlight\_rating**: LED High Performance standard, Digital Light optional (Source [23]). Good standard, excellent optional. Score: 0.40.
*   **security\_rating**: Offers advanced security features like fingerprint scanner and enhanced systems (Source [23]). Score: 0.60.
*   **screen\_in**: Large central touchscreen, 11.9 inches standard/optional (Source [1, 2, 9, 23, 26, 33, 36]). Score: 0.80.
*   **infotainment\_ux\_rating**: Latest MBUX system, generally well-regarded but some find touch controls fiddly (Source [1, 2, 3, 6, 26]). Score: 0.60.
*   **smartphone\_connect\_rating**: Wireless Apple CarPlay and Android Auto standard (Source [1, 2, 11, 36]). Score: 1.00.
*   **audio\_rating**: Optional Burmester system is excellent (Source [6, 23]). Standard system is likely average. Score: 0.40.
*   **usb\_ports**: Offers multiple USB-C ports (Source [16]). Score: 0.60.
*   **comfort\_feature\_score**: Heated front seats standard, power seats with memory, dual-zone climate, ambient lighting (Source [1, 6, 12, 23]). PHEV models often have standard rear air suspension (Source [1, 4, 12, 20]). Good level of comfort features. Score: 0.70.
*   **convenience\_feature\_score**: 360-degree camera, self-parking, wireless charging, fingerprint sensor, keyless entry available (Source [23, 35]). Good convenience features. Score: 0.60.
*   **brand\_prestige\_rating**: Mercedes-Benz is a high-prestige brand. Score: 0.80.
*   **model\_prestige\_rating**: C-Class is a core, mid-range model for Mercedes-Benz. Score: 0.00.
*   **trim\_prestige\_rating**: C300e is a high-tech, efficient variant, representing a significant step up from base models, but not a full AMG performance model. Score: -0.20.
*   **interior\_luxury\_rating**: High-quality materials, S-Class inspired design, ambient lighting (Source [1, 2, 3, 6, 12, 26]). Some reviews note mixed material quality in lower areas (Source [2, 3, 26]). Score: 0.70.
*   **interior\_tech\_focus\_rating**: Dominated by large screens, heavy reliance on touch controls, fingerprint scanner, advanced hybrid interface (Source [1, 2, 4, 6, 9, 11, 16, 23, 26, 33, 36]). High tech focus. Score: 0.90.

